== Part A: one-strategy players and single welfare criteria (exact) ==
G2 payoffs (a,b),(a,b'),(a',b),(a',b'): [(Fraction(3, 1), Fraction(-3, 1), Fraction(0, 1)), (Fraction(-1, 1), Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(1, 1)), (Fraction(-2, 1), Fraction(2, 1), Fraction(2, 1)), (Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(-1, 1), Fraction(3, 1))]
no ties: True
v1^m = 1/7 at p = 3/7 ; v2^m = -1/7 at q = 2/7 ; v3^m = 0
u1 - v1 = 7 (p - 3/7)(q - 2/7) on the 1/7 grid: True
so IR^m = {u1 = v1} = {p = 3/7} U {q = 2/7} (u2 = -u1), movers' gains (0,0) on it
  (p,q) = (3/7,0): u = (1/7, -1/7, 15/7)
  (p,q) = (3/7,1): u = (1/7, -1/7, 8/7)
  (p,q) = (0,2/7): u = (1/7, -1/7, 19/7)
  (p,q) = (1,2/7): u = (1/7, -1/7, 5/7)
  (p,q) = (3/7,2/7): u = (1/7, -1/7, 13/7)
  (p,q) = (11/14,2/7): u = (1/7, -1/7, 8/7)
u3 on arm p=3/7: 15/7 - q: True
u3 on arm q=2/7: 19/7 - 2p: True
=> leximin over the movers predicts the whole cross, including (3/7,1) with u = (1/7,-1/7,8/7),
   Pareto-dominated by (3/7,0) with u = (1/7,-1/7,15/7): PO_b fails in a game without ties.
   Leximin over all players: maximizes u3 on the cross: (0,2/7) with u3 = 19/7 (single profile).
G3 payoffs (a,b),(a,b'),(a',b),(a',b'): [(Fraction(4, 1), Fraction(4, 1), Fraction(0, 1)), (Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 1)), (Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(2, 1)), (Fraction(3, 1), Fraction(3, 1), Fraction(3, 1))]
no ties: True
v^m = (Fraction(2, 1), Fraction(2, 1), Fraction(0, 1)) ; B = {(a,b)} (both movers get 4, their maximum, only there)
sorted gains: (a,b) [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(2, 1), Fraction(2, 1)]  (a',b') [Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(3, 1)]  -> (a',b') leximin-better: True
=> the all-player leximin excludes the bliss profile (a,b): not unilateral (M), in a game without ties.
   Nash product over all players: (a,b) gives 0 , (a',b') gives 3

== Part B: attainable gain vectors for the welfarist impossibility (exact) ==
G1': movers' payoffs constant 0, so B = every mixed profile and the movers' gains are (0,0) everywhere;
  player 3's payoff (v3^m = 0) ranges over [0,3] (bilinear, continuous): sample 0 .. 3
G2: on IR^m player 3's payoff ranges over [8/7,15/7] (arm p=3/7) and [5/7,19/7] (arm q=2/7),
  so the attainable gain vectors are (0,0,z), z in [5/7,19/7], a subset of G1''s.
  Any rule choosing from the attainable gain vectors with Sen's contraction property chooses all of
  [5/7,19/7] in G2, since it chooses all of [0,3] in G1'; so it predicts (3/7,1), dominated.

